# Let's use the "_shortest longident without `__`_".

That concludes the talk.[]{pause}.. Wait how can we implement that?

{up=lets-use pause}
## An example with multiple compilation units

{style=display:flex}
----

{style=width:50%}
---
```ocaml
(* def.ml *)
module X = struct
  type t = A
end
```
```ocaml
(* user.ml *)
let x = Def.X.A
```
```ocaml
(* aliaser.ml *)
type t = Def.X.t
```
{style="padding-left:30px"}
---
- `def.ml` defines a type inside a submodule {pause}

- `user.ml` uses a value of the type.

- `aliaser.ml` aliases it, independently. (and is in the load path) {pause}

{.block}
> How should the type of `x` in `user.ml` be printed?
>
> {carousel poll-element #could_connect14}
> >
> > `Def.X.t` [0]{.poll-result}
> >
> > ---
> >
> > `Aliaser.t` [0]{.poll-result}
> >

{exec}
```slip-script
slip.setClass(document.querySelector("#could_connect14"), "revealed-resuslts", true)
```

----

{.block pause style=background-color:pink;font-size:1.2em #finding-all}
Finding the "shortest path" requires to load ALL compilations units to look for an alias of our type.

{focus="~margin:30 finding-all can-be-complex"}

{pause unfocus up=finding-all}
To **approximate** the shortest path, we need to decide what we load, using heuristics, starting from what is accessible.

{style=display:flex}
----

{style=width:50%}
---


```ocaml
(* def.ml *)
module T = struct
  type t = A
end
```
```ocaml
(* aliaser.ml *)
type t = Def.T.t
```
```ocaml
(* lib.ml *)
type t = Aliaser.t
```
```ocaml
(* user.ml *)
open Lib
let x = Def.T.A (* Shortest type? *)
```
{style="padding-left:30px"}
---
- `User` uses a value of type `Def.T.t`, but this time opens `Lib`

- `Lib.t` aliases `Aliaser.t`,

- `Aliaser.t` aliases `Def.T.t`,

{.block pause}
> How should the type of `x` in `user.ml` be printed?
>
> {carousel poll-element #could_connect15}
> >
> > `Def.T.t` [0]{.poll-result}
> >
> > ---
> >
> > `t` [0]{.poll-result}
> >

{exec}
```slip-script
slip.setClass(document.querySelector("#could_connect15"), "revealed-resuslts", true)
```

{pause}

- The compiler currently uses `Def.T.t`!

----

{up pause}
## The history of printing approximately shortest paths

There already exist two implementations of short-paths for OCaml! {pause}

{style=display:flex}
----
### One in the compiler

- Initially written by Jacques Garrigue.

- Performs a breadth-first search in the environment, one level at a time... {pause}

- ... until it finds a suitable candidate.

- Rather simple, but computationally intensive and sometimes provides unsatisfying results.

{pause}

---

### One in merlin

- A steroid-fed upgrade of the one in the compiler.

- Explores a much larger part of the environment... {pause}

- ...while using subtle heuristics to cut useless branches. {pause}

- It is extremely complex, but gives better results, faster. It was meant to be upstreamed but that never happened. {pause}

- It's one of the main pain points when upgrading Merlin to a new version of the
  typer.
----
{pause}

Let's make a third one ! Leo White, who wrote the current implementation in Merlin came up with a new, "simpler", design.


{pause up}
